    {
      "@id": "skills/conduct/security/injection-boundaries.md#provenance-1",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "bean",
      "cat-harness:section": "`steps.*.outputs` is not a class — it is a PIPE",
      "prov:value": "A step output holds whatever the step put in it, so it has no severity of its\nown. It was in the **safe** band above, unconditionally, until 2026-09-26, and\nthe consequence was live rather than theoretical (bean `6bhf`):\n\n```yaml\n- id: version\n  env:\n    INPUT_VERSION: ${{ github.event.inputs.version }}   # free text — FAIL band\n  run: |\n    echo \"$VERSION\" >> \"$GITHUB_OUTPUT\"                 # ...written out\n# a later step:\n  run: |\n    mv *.tgz \"folio-assistant-${{ steps.version.outputs.version }}.tgz\"\n```",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean 6bhf",
        "2026-09-26"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-26"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/conduct/security/injection-boundaries.md#provenance-2",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "bean",
      "cat-harness:section": "`steps.*.outputs` is not a class — it is a PIPE",
      "prov:value": "**This was already written down, four days before the gate was.**\n[`untrusted-input`](../conduct-core/untrusted-input.md) §\"What counts as\nattacker-controlled\" has listed *\"anything derived from them — including a\n`steps.*.outputs.*` that merely passed one through\"* since 2026-09-18, with a\nmeasured example from this very corpus: the staging workflow bound `head.ref` to\n`env` in one step and wrote the **raw** branch to `$GITHUB_OUTPUT`. The gate,\nwritten 2026-09-22, then classified `steps.*.outputs` safe unconditionally. So\nthis is `1wef`'s own lesson one turn further round — *\"somebody had understood\nthis hazard exactly, nothing checked it\"* — except that this time the thing that\ndid not check it was **the check**. When a gate and a skill disagree, one of them\nis a claim nobody tested; find out which before trusting either.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-09-18",
        "2026-09-22"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-18",
        "2026-09-22"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/conduct/security/injection-boundaries.md#provenance-3",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "`steps.*.outputs` is not a class — it is a PIPE",
      "prov:value": "> **The measurement that missed it is the part worth carrying.** This gap was\n> first recorded as *latent*, on the evidence that *\"every `>> $GITHUB_OUTPUT`\n> write of a reason or title is a literal\"*. That was true. It asked about **two**\n> members of the free-text band — a reason and a title — while the **third**, a\n> dispatch input, was the one being written. A measurement scoped to the\n> instances an audit happened to name is exactly as narrow as a fix scoped to\n> them, which is `path-containment`'s lesson in the same bean on the same day.\n> **Enumerate the band, not its examples.**"
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/conduct/security/injection-boundaries.md#provenance-4",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "bean",
      "cat-harness:section": "`script:` blocks are the SECOND surface — decided yes (bean `j0zs`)",
      "prov:value": "### `script:` blocks are the SECOND surface — decided yes (bean `j0zs`)",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean j0zs"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/conduct/security/injection-boundaries.md#provenance-5",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "`script:` blocks are the SECOND surface — decided yes (bean `j0zs`)",
      "prov:value": "`actions/github-script` runs JavaScript, and `${{ }}` is substituted into its\nsource text the same way. The gate read `run:` only until 2026-09-26, on the\nreasoning that `with:` is where values are passed safely — and `script:` lives\n*under* `with:`, so the scope line and the remedy line collided at exactly one\nkey. That collision was the question; **the measurement answered it.**",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-09-26"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-26"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/conduct/security/injection-boundaries.md#provenance-6",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "`script:` blocks are the SECOND surface — decided yes (bean `j0zs`)",
      "prov:value": "`feature-staging.yml` interpolated the **raw fork branch name** into a template\nliteral, in a step whose sibling two jobs down binds the same value to `env:` and\nexplains why. So it was arbitrary JavaScript execution reachable by opening a\nfork PR, capped only by `pull_request`'s read-only token."
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/conduct/security/injection-boundaries.md#provenance-7",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "`script:` blocks are the SECOND surface — decided yes (bean `j0zs`)",
      "prov:value": "> **And the remedy was already in the corpus, in the workflow next door.**\n> `folio-staging.yml` does the same job — comment the staging URL on the PR — and\n> has always written `const slug = process.env.SLUG` and\n> `` **Branch:** \\`${process.env.BRANCH}\\` ``. Two files doing one job, one\n> right and one wrong, and nothing checked which pattern a file used. That is\n> `1wef`'s lesson a third time, in the surface the gate had declared out of\n> scope: **a scope exclusion is a place defects collect, not a place they are\n> absent.**"
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/conduct/security/injection-boundaries.md#provenance-8",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "bean",
      "cat-harness:section": "Archives: `..`-refusal is not containment — check the member TYPE",
      "prov:value": "**Why the name checks cannot substitute**, measured 2026-09-26 against a real\narchive (bean `6bhf`):\n\n```\n-rw-r--r-- root/root  2 2026-09-26 12:13 main.tex        ← accepted\nlrwxrwxrwx root/root  0 2026-09-26 12:13 evil.tex -> /etc/passwd\nhrw-r--r-- root/root  0 2026-09-26 12:13 hard.tex        ← hardlink\n```",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean 6bhf",
        "2026-09-26"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-26"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/conduct/security/injection-boundaries.md#provenance-9",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "Two defects the fix itself carried, both found by running it",
      "prov:value": "### Two defects the fix itself carried, both found by running it\n\n**The member name is everything after the FIFTH field of a `tvzf` line.** Six was\nwritten first, which ate the first word of every name — `sub/a b c.tex` became\n`b c.tex`, so the containment check ran on a path that was never in the archive:\na guard reading clean while examining a fabrication. Re-reading the regex would\nnot have shown it; building a tarball with a space in a member name did,\nimmediately. **Parse a listing against real output, never against its shape as\nyou remember it.**\n\n**A refusal is not a format mismatch.** The unsafe-member `throw` landed in a\nbare `catch { /* might be a single file */ }`, so a refused archive fell through\nto the next format guess with nothing logged — an operator could not tell a\nhostile archive from a file that was not a tarball. Two states collapsed into\none, which is `ci-health`'s rule at a different layer. A distinct error type is\nwhat lets a handler answer 422 for one and carry on for the other."
    }
  ]
